{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"usequery-dependent-query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"useQuery dependent Query"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dependent (or serial) queries depend on previous ones to finish before they can execute. To achieve this, it's as easy as using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":" option to tell a query when it is ready to run:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Get the user\nconst { data: user } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['user', email],\n  queryFn: getUserByEmail,\n})\n\nconst userId = user?.id\n\n// Then get the user's projects\nconst {\n  status,\n  fetchStatus,\n  data: projects,\n} = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['projects', userId],\n  queryFn: getProjectsByUser,\n  // The query will not execute until the userId exists\n  enabled: !!userId,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"projects"},{"type":"text","value":" query will start in:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"status: 'pending'\nisPending: true\nfetchStatus: 'idle'","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As soon as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"user"},{"type":"text","value":" is available, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"projects"},{"type":"text","value":" query will be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":" and will then transition to:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"status: 'pending'\nisPending: true\nfetchStatus: 'fetching'","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once we have the projects, it will go to:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"status: 'success'\nisPending: false\nfetchStatus: 'idle'","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"usequeries-dependent-query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"useQueries dependent Query"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic parallel query - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQueries"},{"type":"text","value":" can depend on a previous query also, here's how to achieve this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Get the users ids\nconst { data: userIds } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['users'],\n  queryFn: getUsersData,\n  select: (users) => users.map((user) => user.id),\n})\n\n// Then get the users messages\nconst usersMessages = useQueries({\n  queries: userIds\n    ? userIds.map((id) => {\n        return {\n          queryKey: ['messages', id],\n          queryFn: () => getMessagesByUsers(id),\n        }\n      })\n    : [], // if userIds is undefined, an empty array will be returned\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQueries"},{"type":"text","value":" return an "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"array of query results"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"a-note-about-performance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A note about performance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dependent queries by definition constitutes a form of "},{"type":"link","href":"./request-waterfalls.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"request waterfall"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which hurts performance. If we pretend both queries take the same amount of time, doing them serially instead of in parallel always takes twice as much time, which is especially hurtful when it happens on a client that has high latency. If you can, it's always better to restructure the backend APIs so that both queries can be fetched in parallel, though that might not always be practically feasible."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the example above, instead of first fetching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getUserByEmail"},{"type":"text","value":" to be able to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getProjectsByUser"},{"type":"text","value":", introducing a new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getProjectsByUserEmail"},{"type":"text","value":" query would flatten the waterfall."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"usequery-dependent-query","text":"useQuery dependent Query","level":2},{"id":"usequeries-dependent-query","text":"useQueries dependent Query","level":2},{"id":"a-note-about-performance","text":"A note about performance","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## useQuery dependent Query\n\nDependent (or serial) queries depend on previous ones to finish before they can execute. To achieve this, it's as easy as using the `enabled` option to tell a query when it is ready to run:\n\n[//]: # 'Example'\n\n```tsx\n// Get the user\nconst { data: user } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['user', email],\n  queryFn: getUserByEmail,\n})\n\nconst userId = user?.id\n\n// Then get the user's projects\nconst {\n  status,\n  fetchStatus,\n  data: projects,\n} = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['projects', userId],\n  queryFn: getProjectsByUser,\n  // The query will not execute until the userId exists\n  enabled: !!userId,\n})\n```\n\n[//]: # 'Example'\n\nThe `projects` query will start in:\n\n```tsx\nstatus: 'pending'\nisPending: true\nfetchStatus: 'idle'\n```\n\nAs soon as the `user` is available, the `projects` query will be `enabled` and will then transition to:\n\n```tsx\nstatus: 'pending'\nisPending: true\nfetchStatus: 'fetching'\n```\n\nOnce we have the projects, it will go to:\n\n```tsx\nstatus: 'success'\nisPending: false\nfetchStatus: 'idle'\n```\n\n## useQueries dependent Query\n\nDynamic parallel query - `useQueries` can depend on a previous query also, here's how to achieve this:\n\n[//]: # 'Example2'\n\n```tsx\n// Get the users ids\nconst { data: userIds } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['users'],\n  queryFn: getUsersData,\n  select: (users) => users.map((user) => user.id),\n})\n\n// Then get the users messages\nconst usersMessages = useQueries({\n  queries: userIds\n    ? userIds.map((id) => {\n        return {\n          queryKey: ['messages', id],\n          queryFn: () => getMessagesByUsers(id),\n        }\n      })\n    : [], // if userIds is undefined, an empty array will be returned\n})\n```\n\n[//]: # 'Example2'\n\n**Note** that `useQueries` return an **array of query results**\n\n## A note about performance\n\nDependent queries by definition constitutes a form of [request waterfall](./request-waterfalls.md), which hurts performance. If we pretend both queries take the same amount of time, doing them serially instead of in parallel always takes twice as much time, which is especially hurtful when it happens on a client that has high latency. If you can, it's always better to restructure the backend APIs so that both queries can be fetched in parallel, though that might not always be practically feasible.\n\nIn the example above, instead of first fetching `getUserByEmail` to be able to `getProjectsByUser`, introducing a new `getProjectsByUserEmail` query would flatten the waterfall.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: dependent-queries\ntitle: Dependent Queries\n---\n\n## useQuery dependent Query\n\nDependent (or serial) queries depend on previous ones to finish before they can execute. To achieve this, it's as easy as using the `enabled` option to tell a query when it is ready to run:\n\n[//]: # 'Example'\n\n```tsx\n// Get the user\nconst { data: user } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['user', email],\n  queryFn: getUserByEmail,\n})\n\nconst userId = user?.id\n\n// Then get the user's projects\nconst {\n  status,\n  fetchStatus,\n  data: projects,\n} = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['projects', userId],\n  queryFn: getProjectsByUser,\n  // The query will not execute until the userId exists\n  enabled: !!userId,\n})\n```\n\n[//]: # 'Example'\n\nThe `projects` query will start in:\n\n```tsx\nstatus: 'pending'\nisPending: true\nfetchStatus: 'idle'\n```\n\nAs soon as the `user` is available, the `projects` query will be `enabled` and will then transition to:\n\n```tsx\nstatus: 'pending'\nisPending: true\nfetchStatus: 'fetching'\n```\n\nOnce we have the projects, it will go to:\n\n```tsx\nstatus: 'success'\nisPending: false\nfetchStatus: 'idle'\n```\n\n## useQueries dependent Query\n\nDynamic parallel query - `useQueries` can depend on a previous query also, here's how to achieve this:\n\n[//]: # 'Example2'\n\n```tsx\n// Get the users ids\nconst { data: userIds } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['users'],\n  queryFn: getUsersData,\n  select: (users) => users.map((user) => user.id),\n})\n\n// Then get the users messages\nconst usersMessages = useQueries({\n  queries: userIds\n    ? userIds.map((id) => {\n        return {\n          queryKey: ['messages', id],\n          queryFn: () => getMessagesByUsers(id),\n        }\n      })\n    : [], // if userIds is undefined, an empty array will be returned\n})\n```\n\n[//]: # 'Example2'\n\n**Note** that `useQueries` return an **array of query results**\n\n## A note about performance\n\nDependent queries by definition constitutes a form of [request waterfall](./request-waterfalls.md), which hurts performance. If we pretend both queries take the same amount of time, doing them serially instead of in parallel always takes twice as much time, which is especially hurtful when it happens on a client that has high latency. If you can, it's always better to restructure the backend APIs so that both queries can be fetched in parallel, though that might not always be practically feasible.\n\nIn the example above, instead of first fetching `getUserByEmail` to be able to `getProjectsByUser`, introducing a new `getProjectsByUserEmail` query would flatten the waterfall.\n"}
